<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
        <RootNamespace>Bennewitz.Ninja.OpenCode.Sdk</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>OpenCode.Sdk</AssemblyName>
        <Description>
            OpenCode-domain half of the SDK: the config surfaces that exist only because
            OpenCode defines them — its five-rung scope ladder, its per-key merge rules, its
            nested tool-to-glob permission map — plus the concrete clients (OpenCodeClient,
            OpenCodeTuiClient).

            The sibling of ClaudeForge.Sdk.Claude, and deliberately shaped the same way: it
            references AgentForge.Sdk and never the reverse. AssemblyLayeringTests enforces
            that from the assembly-name prefix alone, so this project needed no registration
            there — "OpenCode" was already one of the product prefixes shared code may not
            reference.

            Nothing Claude-specific may appear here and nothing here may appear in
            AgentForge.*. Where the two products genuinely agree, the vocabulary lives in
            AgentForge.Abstractions instead (see PermissionOutcome).
        </Description>
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\AgentForge.Sdk\AgentForge.Sdk.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenCode.Sdk.Tests"/>
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
